\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb, amsthm}

% Optional macros for clarity
\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}
\newcommand{\M}{\mathcal{M}}

\title{Homotopy Types and Finiteness Bounds for Definable Arrangements in O-Minimal Structures}
\author{A. Topologist}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the topological classification of arrangements formed by definable sets within an arbitrary o-minimal expansion of a real closed field $\M$. Given a definable family of generalized subspace arrangements $\mathcal{A}_q$ parameterized by $q \in Q$, we prove that the number of distinct homotopy types realized by the arrangements—as well as their complements—is finite. Furthermore, we establish uniform upper bounds on the number of such homotopy types, depending solely on the definable complexity (dimension and degree) of the parameters, independent of the underlying field. 

Our proof relies on a refinement of the definable triangulation theorem tailored to families of intersecting hypersurfaces, combined with a uniform version of Hardt's triviality theorem for definable maps. By analyzing the spectral sequence of the nerve of definable open covers, we obtain explicit estimates for the Betti numbers of these arrangements. As an application, we extend classical results on the finiteness of homotopy types for real algebraic hyperplane arrangements to broader tame geometric contexts, including subanalytic and exponentially definable arrangements.
\end{abstract}

\end{document}
```

